{
  "dom_id": "step-q213498",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider a relation $R$ that is defined on the set $A=\\{3,6,9\\}.$ Given that $R$ is antisymmetric, enter the missing values in the adjacency matrix. \n\n**Note**: If there is not enough information to determine a particular cell's value, then select the question mark $(\\text{?}).$\n\n|  | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $1$ | $0$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $9$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ | $1$ |",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWe are given elements of the adjacency matrix in the following highlighted cells outside the diagonal:\n\n|  | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ |  | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n| $9$ |  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nAs a result, the relation contains the pairs $(6,3)$ and $(9,6).$ Since $R$ must be antisymmetric, we require \n\n$$\n(3,6)\\notin R,\\,(6,9)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nSo, the cells corresponding to these elements must all be $0.$\n\n|  | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $9$ |  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nWe do not have enough information to determine whether $(9,3)$ is an element of $R.$ \n\n|  | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $9$ | $?$ | $1$ | $1$ |",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
